{"id":"ual-earnings-q3-2026","title":"UAL earnings — United Airlines, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-20","html":"<p>Management sees record revenue and firm pricing ahead, and the one thing that could spoil it is jet fuel rising faster than fares can follow.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-10-20. Next report: not found. Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> Strong on revenue (up 17.4%, a record), softer in leisure, where load factor fell 1.3 points.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> Cut in effect. Q4 adjusted EPS $0.50 to $1.50 against the $1.76 analysts expected. No full-year figure was given.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> Jet fuel. Only about 72% of the increase came back through fares.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> No lean. The rebound stalled at $121.87; a daily close above it decides.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $17,875M, up 17.4%. Webull's revenue estimate was $17.91B.</li><li>GAAP EPS $3.14, adjusted EPS $2.54 (a year ago: $2.78). Consensus of $2.57 to $2.60 is an adjusted figure, so compare it with $2.54, a miss by a few cents. Setting the GAAP $3.14 against that consensus mixes two bases and makes it look like a beat. It was not.</li><li>GAAP EPS was lifted by $294M of sale-leaseback gains. Adjusted EPS landed at the bottom of United's own $2.50 to $3.50 range.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> Q4 adjusted EPS $0.50 to $1.50, revenue growth above 20%, fuel about $4.80 a gallon (Oct 16 forward curve). United said analysts assumed about $4.35, and that the $0.45 gap is worth about $1 of EPS.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> Yield up 16.8%, passenger unit revenue up 16.1%. Premium revenue up 17%, Basic Economy up 16%, contracted business revenue up 26%.</li><li><strong>Fuel recovery:</strong> Fuel averaged $3.90 a gallon against $2.43. About 72% was recovered, below the 80% to 90% expected in July. A similar rate is expected in Q4.</li><li><strong>Leisure:</strong> Load factor down 1.3 points, with more softness flagged for Q4.</li><li><strong>2027 margin:</strong> The double-digit pre-tax margin goal now needs lower fuel, lower industry capacity or continued yield discipline. The non-fuel unit cost target of 2% to 3% growth was reaffirmed.</li><li><strong>Capacity:</strong> Industry capacity is expected to speed up in 2027.</li><li><strong>Quote:</strong> &quot;Our employees met the challenge of our ever-changing environment&quot; (Scott Kirby, CEO, press release).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Incomplete fuel recovery:</strong> about 72% recovered against 80% to 90% planned; Q4 fuel about $4.80 a gallon.</li><li><strong>Softer leisure demand:</strong> load factor down 1.3 points, more expected in Q4.</li><li><strong>Faster industry capacity in 2027:</strong> no figure given.</li><li><strong>Rising non-fuel costs:</strong> a new flight attendant contract; headcount 117.9K, up 5.4%.</li><li><strong>FAA limits at Chicago O'Hare</strong> from Q2 2027.</li><li>The 10-Q risk section was not opened: not found.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-jet-fuel-both-ways\">The big worry: jet fuel, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>A real reopening of the Strait of Hormuz. Waller at the Fed says insurance and transfers there add $20 to $30 a barrel.</li><li>Talks holding. Brent fell to $96.54 on Nov 9 on hopes of de-escalation (ENGINE) and was about $92.5 on Nov 11, down from above $110 in late October.</li><li>Refineries coming back. Chevron says about 10% of global refining is offline and that margins should ease after staying high near term.</li><li>Less flying. United's CFO has said marginal routes get cut, and Kirby expects capacity adjustments across the industry in 2027 (CAPA).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>More incidents in the Strait. A tanker hit a mine there on Nov 11 and WTI closed near $87.</li><li>Slow repair of product supply. Exxon's CEO expects normalisation to run well through next year, with clean products stuck in the Middle East and about half of Russia's refining offline.</li><li>Jet fuel running ahead of crude. Singapore jet averaged $169.53 a barrel in September against $89.03 in February (S&amp;P Global).</li><li>No help from producers. None of Exxon, Chevron or Occidental is raising spending.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How United is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>No hedge found. Reuters (via Hydrocarbon Processing, Mar 6) reported the big US carriers largely stopped hedging. United's own position on a primary page: not found.</li><li>Pricing power is real but incomplete: yield rose 16.8% and still covered only 72% of fuel.</li><li>Against Delta: Delta has a refinery benefit of about $0.40 a gallon and guides Q4 fuel near $4.25. United has neither the refinery nor the lower figure.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Q3 capital spending $2,139M. Full-year guide: not found.</li><li>Buybacks only $27M in nine months, against $610M a year earlier.</li><li>Net leverage 2.4x, up from 2.1x. Investment-grade timing slipped to next year at the earliest, by management's own hope.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Scott Kirby, in the seat since May 2020. Not a founder; he was president from August 2016.</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> Edward M. Philip, independent, since May 2021. The chair and CEO roles are split.</li><li><strong>President:</strong> Brett Hart, since May 2020.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Michael Leskinen, since September 2023.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> dual-class shares or a controlling holder: not found on any page opened.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations (Nov 2025 to Nov 2026):</strong> none found. The newest appointment listed is the CFO in 2023.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> A lot of the strategy and the public voice is Kirby's, but there is a separate chair and a long-serving president beside him.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"UAL\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"UAL · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">UAL · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"277.0\" y2=\"296.2\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"284.7\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"4.5\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"244.3\" y2=\"292.8\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"254.0\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"12.9\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"273.7\" y2=\"306.2\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"275.3\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"27.5\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"24.2\" x2=\"24.2\" y1=\"231.4\" y2=\"309.9\" stroke=\"#2F6E